namespace Ponca
{
/*!
  \page fitting_extending_page Extending the Fitting module

  \tableofcontents

  The Fitting module is designed to be extended: new weight kernels, new neighbor filters and new computational objects
  can be defined outside of the library, and combined with the existing classes. This page describes the API they must
  follow (formally given in \ref ponca_concepts), and the mechanisms used to combine them.

  \note Before defining a new computation, check \ref fittingreference_primitiveoverview and
  \ref fittingreference_outputandcapabilities, as the result might already be available.

  \section fitting_newkernel Defining a new weight kernel

  Ponca::DistWeightFilter computes the weight of a neighbor at distance \f$d\f$ from the evaluation position as
  \f$\phi(d/t)\f$, where \f$\phi\f$ is a *weight kernel*. A kernel provides the function and its first and second
  derivatives, and three flags (see \ref concepts_weighting):

  \snippet concepts.hpp WeightKernelConcept

  For instance, the tricube kernel \f$\phi(x) = (1-x^3)^3\f$ is defined as:
  \code
  template <typename _Scalar>
  class TricubeWeightKernel
  {
  public:
      using Scalar = _Scalar;
      static constexpr bool isCompact = true; // phi(x) = 0 for x >= 1
      static constexpr bool isDValid  = true; // df is valid
      static constexpr bool isDDValid = true; // ddf is valid

      PONCA_MULTIARCH inline Scalar f(const Scalar& x) const
      {
          const Scalar v = Scalar(1) - x * x * x;
          return v * v * v;
      }
      PONCA_MULTIARCH inline Scalar df(const Scalar& x) const
      {
          const Scalar v = Scalar(1) - x * x * x;
          return Scalar(-9) * x * x * v * v;
      }
      PONCA_MULTIARCH inline Scalar ddf(const Scalar& x) const
      {
          const Scalar v = Scalar(1) - x * x * x;
          return Scalar(-18) * x * v * v + Scalar(54) * x * x * x * x * v;
      }
  };

  using Filter = Ponca::DistWeightFilter<Point, TricubeWeightKernel<Scalar>>;
  \endcode

  Ponca::DistWeightFilter also computes the derivatives of the weight with respect to the scale and to the evaluation
  position from `df` and `ddf` (Ponca::DistWeightFilter::scaledw(), Ponca::DistWeightFilter::scaled2w(),
  Ponca::DistWeightFilter::spacedw(), Ponca::DistWeightFilter::spaced2w(),
  Ponca::DistWeightFilter::scaleSpaced2w()). These functions are only available when the corresponding flag is
  `true`, and are only used when derivatives are computed (see \ref fitting_derivatives).

  \section fitting_newfilter Defining a new neighbor filter

  Neighbor filters decide how much each point contributes to a fit, and in which frame its coordinates are given to
  the computations. Ponca::DistWeightFilter covers most needs, but other parameters can be taken into account, e.g.
  the normal of the points, a confidence value, or a segmentation label.

  \subsection fitting_newfilterapi Filter API

  The general API of a filter is:

  \snippet concepts.hpp FilterConcept

  A filter derives from a neighborhood frame, either Ponca::CenteredNeighborhoodFrame (the coordinates of a point
  \f$\mathbf{q}\f$ become \f$\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p}\f$, where \f$\mathbf{p}\f$ is the evaluation position) or
  Ponca::GlobalNeighborhoodFrame (coordinates are not modified). Its call operator returns the weight of the point and
  its local coordinates: points with a null weight are ignored. The requirements are detailed in
  \ref concepts_neighborfilter.

  The simplest way to define a new filter is often to derive from an existing one. The following filter ignores the
  neighbors whose normal points to the opposite direction of a reference normal, which helps to analyze thin
  structures whose two sides are close to each other:
  \code
  template <class DataPoint>
  class NormalAgreementFilter
      : public Ponca::DistWeightFilter<DataPoint, Ponca::SmoothWeightKernel<typename DataPoint::Scalar>>
  {
      using Base = Ponca::DistWeightFilter<DataPoint, Ponca::SmoothWeightKernel<typename DataPoint::Scalar>>;

  public:
      using Scalar           = typename Base::Scalar;
      using VectorType       = typename Base::VectorType;
      using WeightReturnType = typename Base::WeightReturnType;

      PONCA_MULTIARCH inline NormalAgreementFilter(const VectorType& p = VectorType::Zero(), Scalar t = Scalar(1),
                                                   const VectorType& n = VectorType::Zero())
          : Base(p, t), m_n(n)
      {
      }

      PONCA_MULTIARCH inline WeightReturnType operator()(const DataPoint& q) const
      {
          WeightReturnType res = Base::operator()(q); // {weight, local coordinates}
          if (q.normal().dot(m_n) < Scalar(0))
              res.first = Scalar(0);
          return res;
      }

  private:
      VectorType m_n;
  };

  using Fit = Ponca::Basket<Point, NormalAgreementFilter<Point>, Ponca::OrientedSphereFit>;
  Fit fit;
  fit.setNeighborFilter({p, t, n});
  \endcode

  \note The derivatives of the weight (`spacedw`, ...) are inherited from Ponca::DistWeightFilter: they ignore the new
  criterion, which is acceptable as long as the weight is piecewise constant with respect to the evaluation position.

  \section fitting_newestimator Defining a new computational object

  The goal of the Fitting module is to provide lightweight, fast and generic algorithms for point cloud processing. It
  mostly focuses on local regression techniques, which are core components of surface reconstruction techniques
  \cite Alexa:2001:Pss \cite Guennebaud:2007:APSS, geometric descriptors \cite Mellado:2012:GLS
  \cite Lejemble:2021:stable and shape analysis techniques \cite lejemble:2020:persistence. Most of these techniques
  share similar computations, and can be combined in several ways. For instance,
  Ponca::NormalDerivativeWeingartenEstimator estimates curvatures from the spatial derivatives of a normal field, and
  can be combined with any normal estimation technique providing these derivatives.

  To combine these small computations without runtime overhead, the library relies on the Curiously Recurring
  Template Pattern (CRTP). In contrast with dynamic polymorphism, the combination of the computational objects is
  resolved and optimized when compiling the program, which in turn requires more memory and time at compile time.
  Classes do not inherit from a base interface defining the API: they follow concepts (see \ref ponca_concepts).

  \subsection crtpestimator Understanding the CRTP in Ponca

  Ponca::Basket is the interface for all the computations. Its signature is:

  \code
  template <class P, class NF,
            template <class, class, typename> class Ext0,
            template <class, class, typename> class... Exts>
  class Basket;
  \endcode

  The first two parameters are the point type and the neighbor filter used throughout the computation. The following
  ones are the computational objects. Through type list unrolling, the basket inherits from all of them: each
  computational object derives from the previous one in the list, the first one deriving from
  Ponca::BasketUnitBase, which stores the neighbor filter, the number of neighbors, the sum of the weights and the
  fit status. For instance, the basket of \ref example_cxx_basic_page,

  \code
  using Fit1 = Basket<MyPoint, WeightFunc, OrientedSphereFit, GLSParam>;
  \endcode

  defines the following class hierarchy:

  \image html crtpexamplehierachy.svg

  \note Some types are aliases that inject their dependencies in the hierarchy. For example,
  Ponca::OrientedSphereFit is defined as `OrientedSphereFitImpl<MeanPosition<MeanNormal<AlgebraicSphere<T>>>>`
  (template parameters omitted): Ponca::MeanPosition, Ponca::MeanNormal and Ponca::AlgebraicSphere appear in the
  hierarchy although they were not explicitly listed by the user.

  Since the basket inherits from all the computational objects, any of their public functions can be called on the
  basket, as long as its name is not hidden by another class of the hierarchy. Inside a computational object, the
  type `Base` refers to the parent class: `Base::m_uc` in Ponca::GLSParam refers to Ponca::AlgebraicSphere::m_uc,
  whereas `Base::addLocalNeighbor` in Ponca::GLSParam refers to Ponca::OrientedSphereFitImpl::addLocalNeighbor, the
  closest definition in the hierarchy.

  To target a specific class of the hierarchy, each computational object provides a *cast function* returning the
  basket seen as an instance of this class (see \ref fitting_cast):
  \code
  const auto& meanNormal = fit.meanNormal(); // the Ponca::MeanNormal part of the basket (non-const version available)
  VectorType n = meanNormal.meanNormalVector();
  \endcode

  \subsection fitting_newestim Computational object API

  \subsubsection fitting_newestimbase Minimal requirements

  A computational object compatible with Ponca::Basket has the following structure:

  \snippet concepts.hpp ComputationalObjectConcept

  - The macro `PONCA_FITTING_DECLARE_DEFAULT_TYPES` (defined in `Ponca/src/Fitting/defines.h`) defines the `Base`
    type (alias for `T`), as well as `Scalar`, `VectorType` and `NeighborFilter`. It opens a `public` section.
  - The functions `init`, `addLocalNeighbor` and `finalize` must call their `Base` version (`Base::init()`,
    `Base::addLocalNeighbor(...)`, `Base::finalize()`), usually first.
  - `addLocalNeighbor` returns `void`, and receives the weight of the neighbor, its coordinates in the local frame of
    the neighbor filter, and the neighbor itself (to access its other attributes, such as its normal).
  - `finalize` returns the status of the fit, usually the one computed by the base classes, possibly downgraded
    (e.g. to Ponca::UNDEFINED when the problem is degenerate).

  For instance, the following object computes the weighted mean distance between the neighbors and the evaluation
  position. As it relies on local coordinates being relative to the evaluation position, it requires a centered
  neighborhood frame:
  \code
  template <class DataPoint, class _NFilter, typename T>
      requires Ponca::HasLocalFrame<_NFilter>
  class MeanDistance : public T
  {
      PONCA_FITTING_DECLARE_DEFAULT_TYPES

  protected:
      Scalar m_sumD{0}; // weighted sum of the distances

  public:
      PONCA_EXPLICIT_BASKET_CAST_OPERATORS(MeanDistance, meanDistance)

      PONCA_MULTIARCH inline void init()
      {
          Base::init();
          m_sumD = Scalar(0);
      }

      PONCA_MULTIARCH inline void addLocalNeighbor(Scalar w, const VectorType& localQ, const DataPoint& attributes)
      {
          Base::addLocalNeighbor(w, localQ, attributes);
          m_sumD += w * localQ.norm();
      }

      PONCA_MULTIARCH inline Ponca::FIT_RESULT finalize() { return Base::finalize(); }

      PONCA_MULTIARCH inline Scalar meanDistanceValue() const { return m_sumD / Base::getWeightSum(); }
  };

  using Fit = Ponca::Basket<Point, Filter, MeanDistance>;
  \endcode

  \note If a computational object is meant to be the first class of the hierarchy (i.e., it does not derive from
  another computational object), it does not need the parameter `T`: Ponca::BasketUnitBase is always inserted first.

  \subsubsection fitting_newcapabilities Requirements and capabilities

  Aggregating small computational objects minimizes code duplication, and makes it easy to combine techniques. For
  instance, Ponca::GLSParam can be combined with any fitting procedure that computes an algebraic sphere
  (Ponca::SphereFit, Ponca::OrientedSphereFit, Ponca::UnorientedSphereFit).

  To check that computational objects are combined correctly, %Ponca uses C++20 concepts: each class states what it
  requires from the rest of the basket. The capability concepts are defined in `Ponca/src/Fitting/concepts.h` (see
  \ref fittingreference_outputandcapabilities), and the concepts related to baskets, filters and points in
  `Ponca/src/Fitting/Basket/concepts.h`, `Ponca/src/Fitting/Filters/concepts.h` and `Ponca/src/Common/concepts.h`.
  For instance, Ponca::GLSParam is declared as:

  \snippet gls.h example_requirements_usage

  with the following requirement macro:

  \snippet gls.h example_requirements

  If the basket does not provide an algebraic sphere before Ponca::GLSParam, the compilation fails and the compiler
  indicates which constraint is not satisfied. For instance, gcc emits:

  \verbatim
ponca/tests/src/test.cpp:192:9:   required from 'void callSubTests() [with Scalar = long double; int Dim = 3]'
ponca/tests/src/test.cpp:252:5:   required from here
ponca/Ponca/src/Fitting/Basket/basket.h:53:19: error: template constraint failure for 'template<class DataPoint, class _NFilter, class T>  requires  ProvidesAlgebraicSphere<T> class Ponca::GLSParam'
   53 |             using type = Ext<P, NF, Aggregate>;
      |                   ^~~~
ponca/Ponca/src/Fitting/Basket/basket.h:53:19: note: constraints not satisfied
  \endverbatim

  The complete output is verbose, but precise: it tells exactly which expression of which requirement is invalid.
  Classes do not declare which concepts they satisfy (apart from their documentation): concepts are checked
  structurally.

  \note The requirements offer a limited protection against combinations of classes providing the same capability:
  two procedures fitting the same primitive compile, and are detected at runtime, when `finalize()` returns
  Ponca::CONFLICT_ERROR_FOUND (see \ref fitting_multiprimitive). To support this mechanism, a procedure computing a
  primitive must check that the primitive is not already valid (`isValid()`) before writing it.

  \subsubsection fitting_cast Providing cast operators

  Because of name hiding, the `Base` type cannot reach every function of every class of the hierarchy. Cast functions
  solve this problem. They are declared with a macro, in a public section of the class:

  \code
  PONCA_EXPLICIT_BASKET_CAST_OPERATORS(ClassName, castName)
  \endcode

  where `ClassName` is the name of the class template and `castName` the name of the generated function (by
  convention, the class name starting with a lowercase letter). The macro assumes that the template parameters are
  named `DataPoint`, `_NFilter` and `T`. A class can declare several cast functions: it must declare the generic
  cast functions required by the capability concepts it satisfies (e.g. `implicitPrimitive` and `projectionOperator`
  for Ponca::ProvidesImplicitPrimitive). These names are given in \ref fittingreference_outputandcapabilities.

  \subsection fitting_newdiff Differentiating a computational object (BasketDiff API)

  Computational objects used in a Ponca::BasketDiff follow the same principle, with an additional template parameter
  giving the differentiation type, and the basket to differentiate as first base class:

  \snippet concepts.hpp ComputationalDerivativesConcept

  - The first class of the derivative hierarchy is Ponca::BasketDiffUnitBase, which derives from the differentiated
    basket, accumulates the derivatives of the weights (`m_dSumW`) and provides `isScaleDer()`, `isSpaceDer()` and
    `derDimension()`.
  - The macro `PONCA_FITTING_DECLARE_DEFAULT_DER_TYPES` defines `ScalarArray` and `VectorArray`, fixed-size arrays
    whose size is the number of derivatives, laid out as described in \ref fitting_derivatives_layout.
  - `addLocalNeighbor` receives the derivatives `dw` of the weight of the neighbor (`dw[0]` with respect to the scale
    when `isScaleDer()`, followed by the `Dim` derivatives with respect to the evaluation position when
    `isSpaceDer()`).
  - Cast functions are declared with `PONCA_EXPLICIT_BASKET_CAST_OPERATORS_DER(ClassName, castName)`, which assumes
    the template parameters `DataPoint`, `_NFilter`, `DiffType` and `T`.

  Ponca::FitScaleDer and Ponca::FitSpaceDer are flags (see Ponca::DiffType), combined with `|` (Ponca::FitScaleSpaceDer
  is their combination).

  \section fitting_extending_gpu Keeping extensions GPU-compatible

  To be usable in CUDA kernels (see \ref ponca_gpu_page), new classes must:
  - declare all their functions with `PONCA_MULTIARCH`,
  - avoid dynamic memory allocation and STL containers in device code,
  - use the math functions through `PONCA_MULTIARCH_STD_MATH(sqrt)` (and similar), which selects the STL or CUDA
    version of the function.
 */
} // namespace Ponca
